<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?>

<!--
  The Windows application manifest, compiled into the executable by build.rs.

  It exists for one reason: to declare the process DPI-unaware, so that Windows
  scales the finished window itself.

  GTK's Win32 backend has no fractional scaling (GNOME/gtk#1036). Widget
  scaling is an integer, so at Windows' 150% setting GTK builds the whole
  layout at 100%, while the font DPI is a separate number that does follow the
  system and lands at 144. Text at 1.5 inside a layout at 1.0 is what makes the
  window come out cramped, with buttons too small for their own labels.

  Declared unaware, GTK is told 96 dpi and lays everything out at 100%, and
  Windows stretches the result to the display's setting. Exact at 200%, where
  the stretch is a whole number, and slightly soft at 150%. Proportionate at
  both, which it is not today.

  This has to be a manifest rather than a call at startup. Awareness is fixed
  before any of our code runs and cannot be changed afterwards, which is what
  lets it win over the SetProcessDpiAwarenessContext that GTK makes for itself:
  that call fails with ERROR_ACCESS_DENIED, and GTK reads back what was set
  instead of insisting.

  The two elements say the same thing to two generations of Windows. Windows 10
  1607 and later read dpiAwareness and ignore dpiAware; anything older reads
  dpiAware. Both spellings also mean "and the process may not change its mind
  later". The plain absence of these elements is unaware too, but a changeable
  one, which would leave GTK's call to succeed.

  Nothing else is declared here on purpose. UTF-8 as the process code page,
  long path support and a supported-OS list are all real manifest settings with
  real effects, and none of them has anything to do with scaling.

  Beware of one thing when editing: an XML comment may not contain two dashes
  in a row, which is the dash this project writes everywhere else. A manifest
  that does not parse is not ignored, it is fatal. Windows refuses to start the
  process at all, with "the side-by-side configuration is incorrect" and no
  clue as to which file it means.
-->

<assembly xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v1" manifestVersion="1.0"
          xmlns:asmv3="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v3">

  <!-- Required, and nothing binds to it: the version a user is shown comes
       from the version resource build.rs writes beside this file, not from
       here. Left at 1.0.0.0 so releases do not have to touch it. -->
  <assemblyIdentity type="win32"
                    name="ChristophCharles.Collomatique.Gtk4"
                    version="1.0.0.0"/>

  <!-- The ordinary answer: run as whoever started it, never ask for
       administrator rights. Only the installer ever needs those. -->
  <trustInfo xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v2">
    <security>
      <requestedPrivileges xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v3">
        <requestedExecutionLevel level="asInvoker" uiAccess="false" />
      </requestedPrivileges>
    </security>
  </trustInfo>

  <asmv3:application>
    <asmv3:windowsSettings>
      <dpiAware
        xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2005/WindowsSettings">false</dpiAware>
      <dpiAwareness
        xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2016/WindowsSettings">unaware</dpiAwareness>
    </asmv3:windowsSettings>
  </asmv3:application>

</assembly>
